# kuzpkg

**kuzpkg** is the package manager used by **Kuznix Linux**. It is a pacman fork, adapted for the Kuznix package format and workflow.

> **Project status:** Active development. kuzpkg is currently being developed and tested on Linux From Scratch (LFS).

## Current status

kuzpkg is a real, working part of the Kuznix build environment. The project is currently maintained by a very small team, and at the moment I do not have additional developers working with me.

Despite that, development is active:

- kuzpkg is being developed and tested on **Linux From Scratch (LFS)**.
- **All packages required by the current LFS build have been packaged with kuzpkg.**
- Packaging work has also started for **BLFS (Beyond Linux From Scratch)**.
- Some BLFS packages are already available in kuzpkg format.
- The package manager is being developed alongside the Kuznix Linux system.

## What is kuzpkg?

kuzpkg provides package management functionality for Kuznix Linux, including package installation, removal, upgrades, dependency handling, package databases, repositories, and package transactions.

It is based on pacman/libalpm concepts, with Kuznix-specific changes and package handling.

## Package format

Kuznix packages use the `.kuzpkg.tar.zst` package format.

The format is designed for the Kuznix package ecosystem while keeping the familiar archive-based package workflow used by pacman.

## LFS and BLFS

One of the main goals of kuzpkg is to make it possible to build and maintain a complete Kuznix system starting from Linux From Scratch.

The current packaging work follows the LFS dependency chain. Packages needed to construct the LFS system are packaged first, followed by additional software from BLFS.

This means kuzpkg is not just a package manager being developed independently of a distribution: it is being developed while building the actual Kuznix system.

## Development

kuzpkg is currently a small project and development is primarily handled by the Kuznix project maintainer. Contributions, testing, bug reports, documentation improvements, and packaging help are welcome.

If you are interested in helping with development or packaging, open an issue or pull request.

